#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:188
msgid "Regarding Buffer.allocUnsafe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:190
msgid "Be extra cautious when using `Buffer.allocUnsafe`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:191
msgid "Don't use it if you don't have a good reason to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:192
msgid ""
"e.g. you probably won't ever see a performance difference for small "
"buffers, in fact, those might be even faster with `Buffer.alloc()`,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:194
msgid ""
"if your code is not in the hot code path — you also probably won't notice"
" a difference,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:195
msgid "keep in mind that zero-filling minimizes the potential risks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:196
msgid ""
"If you use it, make sure that you never return the buffer in a partially-"
"filled state,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:197
msgid ""
"if you are writing to it sequentially — always truncate it to the actuall"
" written length"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:199
msgid ""
"Errors in handling buffers allocated with `Buffer.allocUnsafe` could "
"result in various issues, ranged from undefined behaviour of your code to"
" sensitive data (user input, passwords, certs) leaking to the remote "
"attacker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Porting-Buffer.md:203
msgid ""
"_Note that the same applies to `new Buffer` usage without zero-filling, "
"depending on the Node.js version (and lacking type checks also adds DoS "
"to the list of potential problems)._"
msgstr ""

